<section class="block on-black">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head"><h2>Six things you can expect from a Social Intent report</h2></div>
    <div class="bento">
      <div class="tile tile-yellow span-4"><h3>You always know what kind of statement you're reading</h3><p>Evidence, stakeholder view, our interpretation, or a decision for the board. Each is labelled, so nothing is dressed up as more certain than it is.</p></div>
      <div class="tile tile-outline span-4"><h3>Every finding carries a confidence rating</h3><p>And a section on what the review could not establish. If we don't know, we say so.</p></div>
      <div class="tile tile-outline span-4"><h3>Challenge is aimed at systems, not people</h3><p>Said to the board as much as to the executive. The point is a better decision, not a scapegoat.</p></div>
      <div class="tile tile-outline span-4"><h3>Our own interest is declared</h3><p>If we think we could help with the next stage, it is written in the report, with advice to test the market before you decide.</p></div>
      <div class="tile tile-outline span-4"><h3>It ends with decisions, not a list</h3><p>A decision sheet, a 90-day plan and a draft minute block, so the work is used rather than filed.</p></div>
      <div class="tile tile-white span-4"><h3>It is honest about what is still emerging</h3><p>A case that overclaims is worse than no case. Funders can tell.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block on-yellow">
